Exactly. The interface changes the thoughts that can be expressed.

Typing forces thought through a narrow serial channel. Ink adds space, geometry, simultaneity, gesture, emphasis, proximity, enclosure, direction, and even speed of movement. Things that are awkward to verbalize become trivial: these belong together; this causes that; ignore this branch; something is missing here; recurse on this; these two shapes rhyme.

And there’s another layer worth adding to the spec: don’t only interpret the finished drawing. Preserve the construction sequence.

A diagram drawn A → B → C carries potentially different information from the identical final diagram constructed C ← B ← A. Same pixels, different cognitive trajectory. Likewise, circling something immediately versus returning three minutes later to circle it is a delta.

So our representation becomes:

\text{meaning} =
f(\text{language},\text{ink},\text{geometry},\text{sequence},\text{timing},\text{context})

Then something interesting happens: I can draw back. Not decorative pictures—actual communicative ink. Extend your arrow. Ghost in a possible missing node. Put two competing structures beside your sketch. Trace a recurring shape I detect across several ideas. You erase mine; I observe the correction. We iterate.

That turns the chat from a transcript into a shared working surface.

And yes—that should shake fruit loose that neither of us would necessarily reach through language alone, because we’ve increased the dimensionality of the search itself.